## 6. Evidence record and status semantics

Create a timestamped evidence directory in the clean account and retain:

- host/Lima inventory and clean-state listing;
- workflow URL/run ID, commit/tag, archive hashes, full tar listing, manifest,
  and checksum results;
- browser name/version and `curl --version`;
- separate xattr output plus exit status for archive, directory, and binary in
  both download paths;
- terminal transcript and any Gatekeeper UI screenshots for first run;
- launcher status/diagnose output, Lima list/config, guest kernel/arch,
  canary, service status, HTTP result, and hello capability response;
- browser/HTTP/WS lab evidence, console transcript, and ping summary;
- before/after upgrade identity hashes and pinned-kernel output;
- non-destructive removal/reinstall proof and final destructive-delete proof;
- a `summary.md` with exactly PASS, NOT RUN, or FAIL for every Â§8 row and links
  to the supporting files.

Status meanings:

- **PASS**: every sub-observation in the criterion was executed successfully
  against the workflow-produced archive on real Apple Silicon where the
  criterion calls for hardware.
- **NOT RUN**: no meaningful attempt was made, including unavailable access or
  missing owner-supplied test input.
- **FAIL**: it was attempted and any required sub-observation failed or could
  not be completed. A later code/doc fix does not turn FAIL into PASS until the
  corrected workflow artifact is rerun through that criterion.

## 7. Verbatim clean-machine hardware qualification

Commands below run in Terminal as the fresh interactive user unless explicitly
prefixed â€œfrom Windowsâ€ or â€œthrough the existing SSH accountâ€. Replace only
`<draft-release-url>`, `<run-id>`, `<clean-user>`, and the recorded archive
filenames. Do not change the machine/profile midway.

### 7.1 Preflight and isolation

1. From Windows, confirm the documented host is reachable:

   ```powershell
   ssh -i ".m5-ssh/iolbox_mac_m0" rohansharma@192.168.101.166 "sw_vers; uname -m; /opt/homebrew/bin/limactl --version"
   ```

2. Interactively log into the fresh account. Record the inventory and prove
   the relevant state paths are absent. If any exists, stop and have the owner
   replace/reset the account; do not clean a questionable account and call it
   fresh.
3. Confirm other-user VMs are not consuming enough memory to invalidate the
   run. On this 8 GB Mac, use `top -l 1 -s 0 | grep PhysMem`; raw free pages
   from `vm_stat` are misleading. Leave `iol22` untouched.

### 7.2 Browser download, quarantine, checksum, and first run

1. In Safari or Chrome in the clean account, open the draft release and
   download `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz` and its `.sha256` file. Record browser
   name/version and whether the browser auto-extracted the tarball. If it did,
   retain/locate the original tarball and record xattrs on both forms.
2. Before changing any xattr, record `xattr -l` and
   `xattr -p com.apple.quarantine` output plus exit status for the archive,
   extracted directory if present, and extracted `iolbox` if present.
3. Verify `shasum -a 256 -c iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256`. Extract with
   `tar -xzf` if the browser did not. Change into `iolbox-macos-arm64` and run
   `shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS`.
4. Record the extracted xattrs again. Apply only the exact user-consent action
   in Â§4 when quarantine is present, record it and its exit status, and record
   the resulting binary xattr state.
5. Run exactly `./iolbox start`, with no asset, payload, machine, profile, or
   Lima override. Save the full output and exit status. This single command
   must create the default durable VM, provision the exact bundled payload,
   reach real HTTP readiness, and open the GUI.
6. Do not confuse liveness with readiness. Require launcher success, an HTTP
   status below 500 at `http://127.0.0.1:4001/`, a rendered GUI, active guest
   service, a passing live canary, and expected guest/kernel/profile facts.
   â€œLima READYâ€ or `systemctl active` alone is insufficient.
7. Run `./iolbox status` and `./iolbox diagnose`. Through the existing SSH
   route, use `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl`, never bare `limactl`, for supporting
   inspection. Confirm GUI/console/capture are host-loopback only and guest
   control is not forwarded.

### 7.3 Real x86_64 IOL lab

1. After the one-download path has reached the GUI, stage one owner-supplied,
   legally held x86_64 IOL image as a test input; it is not part of the archive
   or prerequisite claim. Upload/register it through the rendered GUI.
2. Create a two-node x86_64 IOL lab, give each node `ram: 1024`, link them,
   save it, and start it. Never use `ram: 256`; it can report running while IOS
   is wedged by allocation failures.
3. Wait for real console readiness, not merely node `running`. Configure
   addresses and perform bidirectional traffic. Console connections replay a
   ring buffer, so use the last complete ping summary. Wake IOS consoles,
   disable pagination before long output, and wait for the final prompt.
4. Save console transcripts and a final no-loss-or-documented-first-ARP-loss
   ping result. Confirm the hello/capability UI remains honest: x86_64 is
   usable, i386 is absent/disabled, and no arm64-native IOL support is implied.
5. If browser-equivalent automation is used for evidence, first obtain the
   session cookie and send the matching `Origin` on every WebSocket route. A
   raw unauthenticated WS dial is invalid evidence.

### 7.4 Full-artifact upgrade

1. The preceding start/lab steps must initially use the recorded baseline
   archive built from `cf19f2f`. Before upgrade, record:
   - SHA-256 and listings for host image/lab files;
   - SHA-256/listings for guest `/opt/iolbox/images` and `/opt/iolbox/labs`;
   - guest `hostid`, hostname, `/opt/iolbox/iourc` SHA-256, supervisor version,
     `uname -r`, kernel-policy/structural-gate records, and Lima machine name;
   - a saved lab reload and successful traffic result.
2. Download the M6 candidate archive through the `curl` path in Â§7.5, verify
   it, extract it into a separate directory, and from that candidate directory
   run `./iolbox upgrade` with no path overrides.
3. Require exit 0, active service, live canary PASS, HTTP readiness, and the
   expected new supervisor/archive identity. Fully reload the browser after
   the supervisor restart before diagnosing WebSocket failure; M5 showed the
   app WS can appear disconnected until a page reload even when HTTP and a raw
   WS are healthy.
4. Re-record every value from step 1. Image bytes/list, lab bytes/list, hostid,
   hostname, iourc hash, machine name, and exact pinned `uname -r` must match.
   The saved lab must reload and pass traffic again. A mere binary version
   change is not full-artifact upgrade proof.

### 7.5 `curl` download and quarantine path

1. In a separate empty download directory, run:

   ```sh
   curl -fL -O <draft-release-url>/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz
   curl -fL -O <draft-release-url>/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256
   ```

2. Before extraction, record the same archive xattr commands and exit statuses
   used for the browser download. Verify the outer checksum, extract, verify
   internal checksums, and record directory/binary xattrs. Do not assume curl
   adds or omits quarantine.
3. Apply Â§4 only if the binary is quarantined. Run `./iolbox status` and use
   this extracted candidate for the upgrade in Â§7.4. This proves the curl
   archive is executable and complete without pretending it is another fresh
   account.

### 7.6 Non-destructive removal, recovery, and destructive deletion

1. Run `./iolbox stop` and require exit 0. Verify the named Lima VM is stopped,
   not deleted, and host images/labs remain.
2. Move only the extracted launcher/archive directories to Trash. Do not touch
   `~/.lima`, `~/.iolbox`, or `~/Library/Application Support/iolbox`. Verify the
   VM and data still exist. This is plain launcher removal.
3. Re-extract the verified candidate archive, apply the already-proven
   quarantine procedure if applicable, and run `./iolbox start`. Require the
   same machine, image, saved lab, licence hash, pinned kernel, and successful
   traffic. This proves launcher removal was non-destructive.
4. Only after all other evidence is sealed, demonstrate the separately labelled
   destructive path: stop iolbox; show the exact default machine and data paths;
   delete that one machine with `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl delete
   iolbox-debian13`; move the explicit
   `~/Library/Application Support/iolbox` data directory and the matching
   `~/.iolbox/macos/iolbox-debian13-structural-gate.json` attestation to Trash.
   Never use a glob or broad recursive delete. Record that the VM is absent and
   the data is in Trash/recoverable until emptied.
5. The user documentation must present step 2 as ordinary uninstall and step 4
   as an optional, clearly warned destructive reset. `stop` is never described
   as uninstall or deletion.

## 8. Acceptance criteria

The implementation result copies this table and assigns exactly one status to
each row.

| # | Criterion required for PASS | Required evidence |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | CI executes launcher vet/unit tests, compiles Darwin-selected tests, and cross-builds `CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64`; the workflow-produced binary also starts/diagnoses on the real Mac. | Green CI job/log, workflow artifact provenance, `file` output, real-Mac command transcript. |
| 2 | The release workflow emits exactly `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz` plus its outer checksum; the archive has exactly Â§2.2, all internal checksums pass, its native payload is byte-identical to the same run's `build-linux` payload, and a second identical pack has the same hash. | Workflow log/artifacts, two hashes, extracted manifest, payload hash comparison on the Mac. |
| 3 | A fresh supported Apple Silicon user with only Lima as the separately installed runtime prerequisite reaches the rendered GUI using the browser-downloaded archive and one documented `./iolbox start`. | Clean-state inventory, browser download, launcher/HTTP/browser/service/canary evidence on hardware. |
| 4 | Browser download quarantine is recorded before mutation and the exact documented, narrow first-run Gatekeeper procedure succeeds without signing, notarization, global disablement, or automatic clearing. | xattr exit/output for archive/directory/binary, Gatekeeper screenshot/transcript, successful first run. |
| 5 | The `curl` path is independently downloaded, checksum-verified, extracted, xattr-checked, and successfully used on the Mac; presence or absence of quarantine is reported rather than assumed. | curl transcript/version, separate hashes/listing/xattrs, real command result. |
| 6 | A real owner-supplied x86_64 IOL image runs in a saved two-node lab and passes bidirectional traffic through the shipped artifact; i386 and arm64-native IOL are not advertised as supported. | Rendered GUI/hello evidence, console transcripts, last complete ping summaries, saved-lab proof. |
| 7 | `./iolbox upgrade` from the `cf19f2f` baseline archive to the candidate preserves host and guest images/labs, hostid/hostname, exact iourc hash, Lima machine identity, and pinned guest kernel; the saved lab reloads and passes traffic afterward. | Complete before/after identity table and post-upgrade browser/traffic evidence. |
| 8 | Plain launcher removal leaves the stopped VM and data intact and re-extraction recovers the same lab; the separately warned destructive sequence deletes only the named VM/data and is demonstrated last. | Lima/data listings before removal, after removal, after recovery, and after destructive cleanup. |
| 9 | `docs/INSTALL.md`, archive README, provider docs, third-party notice, maintainer release doc, and draft release notes consistently name Lima, actual supported macOS/profile and guest/kernel matrix, Rosetta-amd64, x86_64-IOL-only, unsigned/Gatekeeper, loopback security, upgrade, and uninstall boundaries. | Exact published draft text/archive notice reviewed against the workflow artifact and real observations; no unsupported capability claim. |

If a criterion is rerun after a fix, retain the failed evidence and identify the
new workflow run/archive hash that earned PASS. Never relabel the earlier run.

## 9. Risks and assumptions

1. **M4 remains PARTIAL and is out of scope.** Only items 1 and 6 were proven
   on hardware; items 2 and 7 have unit-tested fixes not hardware-reconfirmed;
   items 3, 4, 5, and 8 were not attempted. M6 does not proactively execute or
   close that backlog. Because M6 packages and runs the cumulative M1â€“M5
   product, an unresolved M4 defect may surface naturally in the clean install,
   lab, upgrade, or uninstall run. Root-cause and record it. Fix M6 packaging/
   docs defects in scope; do not turn the session into an M4 backlog hunt. If
   the defect blocks an M6 criterion, that criterion is FAIL until resolved and
   rerun.
2. **M5 criterion 2 is NOT ATTEMPTED and out of scope by owner direction.** No
   ordinary amd64 non-Mac target was available in the authoritative M5 sessions
   for this planning task. M6 neither acquires one nor repeats that criterion.
   Repository text that suggests otherwise is superseded for this plan by the
   owner-supplied scope. M6 still documents the Mac's x86_64-only capability
   honestly; it does not use release qualification to infer non-Mac behavior.
3. **Fresh-account assumption.** The plan assumes the owner can provide a new
   interactive account on `192.168.101.166` while retaining the installed Lima
   binary. This gives clean per-user state without depending on a new physical
   Mac. If interactive browser access or a truly empty home cannot be provided,
   criteria 3â€“5 are NOT RUN. The used `rohansharma` account is not an acceptable
   silent fallback.
4. **Quarantine varies by browser, settings, extraction path, and macOS.** A
   browser may auto-extract, propagate quarantine differently, or Gatekeeper
   may present a different UI than expected. The plan records all layers before
   mutation and forbids synthetic xattrs. The candidate `xattr -d` procedure is
   deliberately file-scoped, but it must be replaced everywhere if hardware
   proves a different user action is the successful path.
5. **Unsigned provenance remains limited.** Internal and outer hashes detect
   mismatch but, when hosted beside the unsigned artifact, do not authenticate
   the publisher. Release and install docs must say the artifact is unsigned;
   M6 has no hidden signing/notarization work.
6. **Exact-payload coupling is a release risk.** Rebuilding the native payload
   independently in `build-macos`, selecting â€œnewestâ€, or allowing multiple
   tarballs could ship different bits from the native-systemd artifact. The Mac
   job therefore consumes and hash-compares the exact `build-linux` output from
   the same run.
7. **External downloads can fail.** Lima and the pinned guest image are not in
   the archive. A moved image URL, unavailable mirror, stale Homebrew Lima
   bottle, or Rosetta share warning can block clean provisioning. Keep the
   structural canary and digest refusal intact. `brew upgrade lima` is not
   remediation for a stale same-version bottle; the known action is
   `brew reinstall lima`, performed by the user outside iolbox.
8. **The 8 GB Mac is resource-constrained and already has other-user VMs.**
   Stop only owner-approved reusable machines if needed and never touch
   `iol22`. Use 1024 MB per IOL node and record host memory pressure so a
   resource failure is not mislabelled as archive corruption.
9. **Browser automation has protocol traps.** WebSocket evidence needs the
   HTTP session cookie plus same-origin `Origin`; consoles replay their ring
   buffer; and after supervisor restart the app may need a full page reload.
   Carry these M3/M5 facts forward before calling a product failure.
10. **Lima configuration has a known quoting trap.** If qualification
    diagnostics must inspect or reproduce a `limactl --set` operation, yq map
    keys in the expression must remain quoted. Do not â€œsimplifyâ€ the locked
    template or a working launcher-generated expression while debugging the
    archive. Record the rendered per-machine Lima YAML before blaming the
    provisioner.
11. **Shared-worktree contamination is possible.** Earlier phases observed a
    concurrent session changing and committing files in the same worktree.
    Recheck branch/HEAD/status at every handoff, preserve the pre-existing
    `docs/m6-session-logs/` tree, and stage exact paths only.
12. **Schedule confidence is low.** The master estimate is 1.5â€“2 focused days,
    including one clean-machine pass. Every earlier Mac phase exceeded its
    estimate once real hardware exposed defects that CI/static review missed.
    Reserve an uninterrupted genuine clean-account hardware pass plus time for
    at least one corrected workflow rebuild/rerun; CI-green is only the entry
    gate, not schedule completion.

## 10. Explicit non-goals

- M4 backlog closure or proactive M4 regression hunting.
- M5 criterion 2 or acquisition of an amd64 Linux/CI target.
- Code signing, notarization, entitlements, a `.app`, `.dmg`, Homebrew formula,
  package installer, or automatic Gatekeeper/quarantine changes.
- Installing, upgrading, reinstalling, or uninstalling Lima for the user.
- A guest disk or bundled hypervisor.
- Tauri/Rust shell repair or release packaging.
- i386/i86bi enablement, arm64-native IOL, native-arm64 supervisor/VPCS,
  FEX/qemu-user, or any other M7 work.
- Changes to launcher lifecycle, sync, ports, GUI, supervisor, native payload,
  or guest provisioning behavior unless the later implementation session finds
  a defect directly preventing an M6 acceptance criterion and separately
  obtains the appropriate scope decision.

## 11. Estimate and handoff condition

