   1: # M6 plan — build, distribute, and qualify the unsigned Apple Silicon archive
   2: 
   3: Status: planning only. No M6 implementation has been attempted.
   4: 
   5: Branch/worktree baseline: `luna/macos-m6-followups` at `cf19f2f`, in
   6: `J:\Claude code\iolab-m6-wt`. M6 depends on the already-implemented M1–M5
   7: stack on this branch. It does not depend on those ancestor branches being
   8: merged to `main` before implementation begins.
   9: 
  10: ## 1. Scope and completion rule
  11: 
  12: M6 packages and qualifies what M1–M5 already built. It is not a launcher,
  13: guest, runtime, or capability feature phase. Its goal is one repeatably built,
  14: unsigned `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz` whose only separately installed runtime
  15: prerequisite is Lima. iolbox detects and uses Lima; it does not install,
  16: upgrade, reinstall, or remove Lima.
  17: 
  18: The immutable scope is `docs/macos-arm64-plan.md` §M6. In particular:
  19: 
  20: - add launcher CI coverage for `darwin/arm64`;
  21: - add a Mac archive job to the existing release pipeline without changing the
  22:   other target artifacts or reviving the abandoned Tauri/Rust shell;
  23: - package the Go launcher, the exact native Linux payload built by the same
  24:   workflow, the existing locked Lima profiles/templates, the existing guest
  25:   provisioner, checksums, and notices;
  26: - add the seventh release-artifact row/section to the current install guide
  27:   and make the provider and third-party disclosures accurate;
  28: - qualify browser and `curl` downloads, Gatekeeper/quarantine behavior, one
  29:   command to the GUI, a real x86_64 IOL lab, full-artifact upgrade, and both
  30:   non-destructive and destructive uninstall meanings on Apple Silicon;
  31: - ship unsigned and document the user-consent path through Gatekeeper. Do not
  32:   sign, notarize, clear quarantine automatically, disable Gatekeeper, or use
  33:   `spctl --master-disable`;
  34: - do no M7 work. Native-arm64 supervisor/VPCS plus FEX/qemu-user is a separate,
  35:   independent phase and is neither a prerequisite nor a fallback for M6.
  36: 
  37: M6 may be called PASS only when every criterion in §8 is PASS. A green CI run,
  38: successful cross-compile, archive listing, static review, or unit test is not
  39: substituted for the required real-hardware observations.
  40: 
  41: ## 2. Release design decisions
  42: 
  43: ### 2.1 Artifact boundary
  44: 
  45: The release contains no hypervisor and no guest disk. The user installs Lima;
  46: Lima downloads the image named and digest-locked by the shipped profile. The
  47: archive contains no Cisco software. Users supply appropriately licensed
  48: x86_64 IOL/IOU images.
  49: 
  50: The binary is named `iolbox`, so the documented first command is:
  51: 
  52: ```sh
  53: ./iolbox start
  54: ```
  55: 
  56: No `--assets-dir` or `--tarball` is needed in the release layout. The current
  57: Darwin launcher finds `lima/profiles.env` beside its executable and searches
  58: that same asset-root directory (non-recursively) for
  59: `iolbox-server-*.tar.gz`. The native tarball therefore must sit beside
  60: `iolbox`, not in a `payload/` subdirectory. That existing contract determines
  61: the archive layout; M6 does not redesign it merely to mirror the older
  62: conceptual diagram in the master plan.
  63: 
  64: `packaging/macos/iolbox-mac.sh` and `packaging/macos/tests/` do not ship. The
  65: shell entry point was the M1 host driver and is superseded for users by the Go
  66: launcher; the test harnesses are qualification source, not runtime material.
  67: The complete guest scripts and every file referenced by `profiles.env` do
  68: ship.
  69: 
  70: ### 2.2 Exact archive layout
  71: 
  72: The gzip member has one top-level directory and no files beside it:
  73: 
  74: ```text
  75: iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz
  76: └── iolbox-macos-arm64/
  77:     ├── iolbox                                      mode 0755; darwin/arm64
  78:     ├── README.md                                   mode 0644; Mac quick start
  79:     ├── LICENSE                                     mode 0644; iolbox license
  80:     ├── SHA256SUMS                                  mode 0644; every file below except itself
  81:     ├── iolbox-server-<release-version>.tar.gz      mode 0644; exact build-linux output
  82:     ├── lima/
  83:     │   ├── profiles.env
  84:     │   ├── iolbox-trixie.yaml
  85:     │   ├── iolbox-jammy.yaml
  86:     │   ├── iolbox-bookworm.yaml
  87:     │   ├── pinned-image-debian13.env
  88:     │   ├── pinned-image.env
  89:     │   └── pinned-image-debian12.env
  90:     ├── guest/
  91:     │   ├── lib.sh
  92:     │   ├── 10-multiarch-debian.sh
  93:     │   ├── 10-multiarch.sh
  94:     │   ├── 20-kernel-hold-debian.sh
  95:     │   ├── 20-kernel-hold.sh
  96:     │   ├── 30-canary.sh
  97:     │   ├── 40-install-payload.sh
  98:     │   └── 50-verify.sh
  99:     └── notices/
 100:         └── THIRD_PARTY.md
 101: ```
 102: 
 103: The three profile/template/pin sets are intentional. Debian 13/trixie is the
 104: current DEFAULT and is pinned; Ubuntu 22.04/Jammy is the pinned COMPATIBILITY
 105: profile; Debian 12/bookworm remains an unqualified CANDIDATE which the current
 106: launcher refuses while its digest is unpinned. Omitting the candidate files
 107: would make the shipped `profiles.env` internally incomplete; including them
 108: does not advertise candidate support.
 109: 
 110: `SHA256SUMS` uses paths relative to `iolbox-macos-arm64/`, sorted bytewise and
 111: generated after permissions are normalized. The release also attaches
 112: `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256` next to the archive so a user can verify the
 113: download before extraction. That outer checksum is not inside the archive.
 114: Checksums published in the same unsigned release provide corruption/tamper
 115: detection after publication, not cryptographic publisher identity; the docs
 116: must not describe them as a substitute for signing.
 117: 
 118: All directories in the archive have mode 0755. No symlinks, device nodes,
 119: hard links, extended attributes, AppleDouble files, or resource forks are
 120: permitted in the tar member set.
 121: 
 122: ### 2.3 Repeatability and payload identity
 123: 
 124: Add one manifest as the allow-list for the archive. The packer must fail on a
 125: missing manifest member, an unexpected staged member, more or fewer than one
 126: native payload tarball, a non-regular/symlinked launcher or payload, a launcher
 127: which is not Mach-O arm64, or a payload whose hash differs from the artifact
 128: downloaded from `build-linux`.
 129: 
 130: Normalize file modes, owner/group metadata, member ordering, and timestamps
 131: from `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`; create gzip output without an embedded current-time
 132: header. A packaging test builds twice from identical inputs and requires
 133: identical archive SHA-256 values. The archive filename stays unversioned as
 134: required; the GitHub tag, embedded launcher version, native payload filename,
 135: release metadata, and checksums carry the version identity.
 136: 
