 561: ## 9. Risks and assumptions
 562: 
 563: 1. **M4 remains PARTIAL and is out of scope.** Only items 1 and 6 were proven
 564:    on hardware; items 2 and 7 have unit-tested fixes not hardware-reconfirmed;
 565:    items 3, 4, 5, and 8 were not attempted. M6 does not proactively execute or
 566:    close that backlog. Because M6 packages and runs the cumulative M1–M5
 567:    product, an unresolved M4 defect may surface naturally in the clean install,
 568:    lab, upgrade, or uninstall run. Root-cause and record it. Fix M6 packaging/
 569:    docs defects in scope; do not turn the session into an M4 backlog hunt. If
 570:    the defect blocks an M6 criterion, that criterion is FAIL until resolved and
 571:    rerun.
 572: 2. **M5 criterion 2 is NOT ATTEMPTED and out of scope by owner direction.** No
 573:    ordinary amd64 non-Mac target was available in the authoritative M5 sessions
 574:    for this planning task. M6 neither acquires one nor repeats that criterion.
 575:    Repository text that suggests otherwise is superseded for this plan by the
 576:    owner-supplied scope. M6 still documents the Mac's x86_64-only capability
 577:    honestly; it does not use release qualification to infer non-Mac behavior.
 578: 3. **Fresh-account assumption.** The plan assumes the owner can provide a new
 579:    interactive account on `192.168.101.166` while retaining the installed Lima
 580:    binary. This gives clean per-user state without depending on a new physical
 581:    Mac. If interactive browser access or a truly empty home cannot be provided,
 582:    criteria 3–5 are NOT RUN. The used `rohansharma` account is not an acceptable
 583:    silent fallback.
 584: 4. **Quarantine varies by browser, settings, extraction path, and macOS.** A
 585:    browser may auto-extract, propagate quarantine differently, or Gatekeeper
 586:    may present a different UI than expected. The plan records all layers before
 587:    mutation and forbids synthetic xattrs. The candidate `xattr -d` procedure is
 588:    deliberately file-scoped, but it must be replaced everywhere if hardware
 589:    proves a different user action is the successful path.
 590: 5. **Unsigned provenance remains limited.** Internal and outer hashes detect
 591:    mismatch but, when hosted beside the unsigned artifact, do not authenticate
 592:    the publisher. Release and install docs must say the artifact is unsigned;
 593:    M6 has no hidden signing/notarization work.
 594: 6. **Exact-payload coupling is a release risk.** Rebuilding the native payload
 595:    independently in `build-macos`, selecting “newest”, or allowing multiple
 596:    tarballs could ship different bits from the native-systemd artifact. The Mac
 597:    job therefore consumes and hash-compares the exact `build-linux` output from
 598:    the same run.
 599: 7. **External downloads can fail.** Lima and the pinned guest image are not in
 600:    the archive. A moved image URL, unavailable mirror, stale Homebrew Lima
 601:    bottle, or Rosetta share warning can block clean provisioning. Keep the
 602:    structural canary and digest refusal intact. `brew upgrade lima` is not
 603:    remediation for a stale same-version bottle; the known action is
 604:    `brew reinstall lima`, performed by the user outside iolbox.
 605: 8. **The 8 GB Mac is resource-constrained and already has other-user VMs.**
 606:    Stop only owner-approved reusable machines if needed and never touch
 607:    `iol22`. Use 1024 MB per IOL node and record host memory pressure so a
 608:    resource failure is not mislabelled as archive corruption.
 609: 9. **Browser automation has protocol traps.** WebSocket evidence needs the
 610:    HTTP session cookie plus same-origin `Origin`; consoles replay their ring
 611:    buffer; and after supervisor restart the app may need a full page reload.
 612:    Carry these M3/M5 facts forward before calling a product failure.
 613: 10. **Lima configuration has a known quoting trap.** If qualification
 614:     diagnostics must inspect or reproduce a `limactl --set` operation, yq map
 615:     keys in the expression must remain quoted. Do not “simplify” the locked
 616:     template or a working launcher-generated expression while debugging the
 617:     archive. Record the rendered per-machine Lima YAML before blaming the
 618:     provisioner.
 619: 11. **Shared-worktree contamination is possible.** Earlier phases observed a
 620:     concurrent session changing and committing files in the same worktree.
 621:     Recheck branch/HEAD/status at every handoff, preserve the pre-existing
 622:     `docs/m6-session-logs/` tree, and stage exact paths only.
 623: 12. **Schedule confidence is low.** The master estimate is 1.5–2 focused days,
 624:     including one clean-machine pass. Every earlier Mac phase exceeded its
 625:     estimate once real hardware exposed defects that CI/static review missed.
 626:     Reserve an uninterrupted genuine clean-account hardware pass plus time for
 627:     at least one corrected workflow rebuild/rerun; CI-green is only the entry
 628:     gate, not schedule completion.
 629: 
 630: ## 10. Explicit non-goals
 631: 
 632: - M4 backlog closure or proactive M4 regression hunting.
 633: - M5 criterion 2 or acquisition of an amd64 Linux/CI target.
 634: - Code signing, notarization, entitlements, a `.app`, `.dmg`, Homebrew formula,
 635:   package installer, or automatic Gatekeeper/quarantine changes.
 636: - Installing, upgrading, reinstalling, or uninstalling Lima for the user.
 637: - A guest disk or bundled hypervisor.
 638: - Tauri/Rust shell repair or release packaging.
 639: - i386/i86bi enablement, arm64-native IOL, native-arm64 supervisor/VPCS,
 640:   FEX/qemu-user, or any other M7 work.
 641: - Changes to launcher lifecycle, sync, ports, GUI, supervisor, native payload,
 642:   or guest provisioning behavior unless the later implementation session finds
 643:   a defect directly preventing an M6 acceptance criterion and separately
 644:   obtains the appropriate scope decision.
 645: 
 646: ## 11. Estimate and handoff condition
 647: 
 648: Retain the master estimate of 1.5–2 focused days as the no-defect engineering
 649: estimate: roughly half a day for CI/packer/release integration, half a day for
 650: docs/notices and workflow dry run, and at least half to one full day for the
 651: fresh-account browser/curl/lab/upgrade/uninstall sequence. This is not a promise
 652: that the hardware pass will fit the estimate; plan capacity for iterative
 653: artifact rebuilds based on M2–M5 experience.
 654: 
 655: The M6 implementation handoff must name the exact commit, workflow run, archive
 656: SHA-256, clean account/host facts, evidence root, per-criterion status, defects
 657: found, and final machine/data state. It must say PARTIAL unless all §8 rows are
 658: PASS, regardless of how much code or documentation was completed.

