 772: ## 9. Risks and assumptions
 773: 
 774: 1. **M4 remains PARTIAL and is out of scope.** Only items 1 and 6 were proven
 775:    on hardware; items 2 and 7 have unit-tested fixes not hardware-reconfirmed;
 776:    items 3, 4, 5, and 8 were not attempted. M6 does not proactively execute or
 777:    close that backlog. Because M6 packages and runs the cumulative M1–M5
 778:    product, an unresolved M4 defect may surface naturally in the clean install,
 779:    lab, upgrade, or uninstall run. Root-cause and record it. Fix M6 packaging/
 780:    docs defects in scope; do not turn the session into an M4 backlog hunt. If
 781:    the defect blocks an M6 criterion, that criterion is FAIL until resolved and
 782:    rerun.
 783: 2. **M5 is fully PASS; M4 is the only inherited incomplete phase.** M5
 784:    criterion 2 passed on `noble-builder-vm` as recorded in the current M5
 785:    handoff/result. M6 has no amd64 non-Mac exception, inference, or acquisition,
 786:    and no M6 step depends on that criterion remaining open.
 787: 3. **Hardware access is a gate, not an implementation assumption.** §0 requires
 788:    a genuinely fresh account with SSH plus either agent-controlled interactive
 789:    desktop/browser access or owner-executed GUI evidence handoff. If it is not
 790:    met, rows 3–8 are NOT RUN. `rohansharma`, `sudo -iu`, and HTTP-only evidence
 791:    are not silent fallbacks.
 792: 4. **Quarantine varies by browser, settings, extraction path, and macOS.** A
 793:    browser may auto-extract, propagate quarantine differently, or Gatekeeper
 794:    may present a different UI than expected. The plan records all layers before
 795:    mutation and forbids synthetic xattrs. The candidate `xattr -d` procedure is
 796:    deliberately file-scoped, but it must be replaced everywhere if hardware
 797:    proves a different user action is the successful path.
 798: 5. **Unsigned provenance remains limited.** Internal and outer hashes detect
 799:    mismatch but, when hosted beside the unsigned artifact, do not authenticate
 800:    the publisher. Release and install docs must say the artifact is unsigned;
 801:    M6 has no hidden signing/notarization work.
 802: 6. **Exact-payload coupling is a release risk.** Rebuilding the native payload
 803:    independently in `build-macos`, selecting “newest”, or allowing multiple
 804:    tarballs could ship different bits from the native-systemd artifact. The Mac
 805:    job therefore consumes and hash-compares the exact `build-linux` output from
 806:    the same run.
 807: 7. **Draft download authentication is required even though the repo is
 808:    public.** Draft assets are collaborator-only until publication. Reconfirm
 809:    visibility before the session, keep the clean browser authenticated, use
 810:    exact GitHub REST asset IDs for curl, and keep the token out of history,
 811:    tracing, saved headers, and environment dumps. HTML is a failed download.
 812: 8. **External downloads can fail.** Lima and the pinned guest image are not in
 813:    the archive. A moved image URL, unavailable mirror, stale Homebrew Lima
 814:    bottle, or Rosetta share warning can block clean provisioning. Keep the
 815:    structural canary and digest refusal intact. `brew upgrade lima` is not
 816:    remediation for a stale same-version bottle; the known action is
 817:    `brew reinstall lima`, performed by the user outside iolbox.
 818: 9. **The 8 GB Mac is resource-constrained and already has other-user VMs.**
 819:    Stop only owner-approved reusable machines if needed and never touch
 820:    `iol22`. Use 1024 MB per IOL node and record host memory pressure so a
 821:    resource failure is not mislabelled as archive corruption.
 822: 10. **Browser automation has protocol traps.** WebSocket evidence needs the
 823:    HTTP session cookie plus same-origin `Origin`; consoles replay their ring
 824:    buffer; and after supervisor restart the app may need a full page reload.
 825:    Carry these M3/M5 facts forward before calling a product failure.
 826: 11. **Lima configuration has a known quoting trap.** If qualification
 827:     diagnostics must inspect or reproduce a `limactl --set` operation, yq map
 828:     keys in the expression must remain quoted. Do not “simplify” the locked
 829:     template or a working launcher-generated expression while debugging the
 830:     archive. Record the rendered per-machine Lima YAML before blaming the
 831:     provisioner.
 832: 12. **Shared-worktree contamination is possible.** Earlier phases observed a
 833:     concurrent session changing and committing files in the same worktree.
 834:     Recheck branch/HEAD/status at every handoff, preserve the pre-existing
 835:     `docs/m6-session-logs/` tree, and stage exact paths only.
 836: 13. **Schedule confidence is low.** The master estimate is 1.5–2 focused days,
 837:     including one clean-machine pass. Every earlier Mac phase exceeded its
 838:     estimate once real hardware exposed defects that CI/static review missed.
 839:     Reserve an uninterrupted genuine clean-account hardware pass plus time for
 840:     at least one corrected workflow rebuild/rerun; CI-green is only the entry
 841:     gate, not schedule completion.
 842: 
 843: ## 10. Explicit non-goals
 844: 
 845: - M4 backlog closure or proactive M4 regression hunting.
 846: - Code signing, notarization, entitlements, a `.app`, `.dmg`, Homebrew formula,
 847:   package installer, or automatic Gatekeeper/quarantine changes.
 848: - Installing, upgrading, reinstalling, or uninstalling Lima for the user.
 849: - A guest disk or bundled hypervisor.
 850: - Tauri/Rust shell repair or release packaging.
 851: - i386/i86bi enablement, arm64-native IOL, native-arm64 supervisor/VPCS,
 852:   FEX/qemu-user, or any other M7 work.
 853: - Changes to launcher lifecycle, sync, ports, GUI, supervisor, native payload,
 854:   or guest provisioning behavior unless the later implementation session finds
 855:   a defect directly preventing an M6 acceptance criterion and separately
 856:   obtains the appropriate scope decision.
